-0.3 (is de\336ned in a library) 189 556.33 P
-0.3 (, the heap will contain at most one copy of each of the) 285.78 556.33 P
(class\325) 189 543.33 T
(s methods, no matter how many applications use objects from that) 213.08 543.33 T
(class. There is another advantage as well; all applications which use that) 189 530.33 T
-0.8 (class can be sure that they are using objects whose de\336nitions are identical.) 189 517.33 P
(this makes it possible for applications to send messages to objects owned) 189 504.33 T
(by other geodes.) 189 491.33 T
(W) 189 471.33 T
(riting a library is very much like writing an application. There are only) 198.26 471.33 T
(a few differences, which are covered in this appendix. Y) 189 458.33 T
(ou should already) 444.38 458.33 T
(be familiar with writing applications before you try to write a library) 189 445.33 T
(.) 506.09 445.33 T
2 14 Q
(C.2) 149.22 393 T
2 24 Q
(Library Basics) 189 393 T
3 10 Q
-0.38 (A library is a geode, much like an application geode. However) 189 367.33 P
-0.38 (, its behavior) 469.15 367.33 P
-0.65 (is slightly different. In particular) 189 354.33 P
-0.65 (, libraries do not have any threads of their) 338.83 354.33 P
(own, unless they explicitly create them.) 189 341.33 T
-0.04 (When a geode calls a routine which is exported from a library) 189 321.33 P
-0.04 (, the routine) 471.31 321.33 P
-0.04 (is run by the thread which made the call, not by the library\325) 189 308.33 P
-0.04 (s thread. This) 464.08 308.33 P
(has several implications. First, it means that a library\325) 189 295.33 T
(s response time is) 441.47 295.33 T
(not dependent on the number of applications which use the library) 189 282.33 T
(. An) 495.69 282.33 T
-0.23 (application which uses the library a lot will do so on its own time and may) 189 269.33 P
-0.19 (have its priority reduced accordingly) 189 256.33 P
-0.19 (. Indeed, a library with many users is) 356.38 256.33 P
-0.12 (likely to perform better than one with few users, since its code will be less) 189 243.33 P
-0.22 (likely to be swapped out of the global heap. Similarly) 189 230.33 P
-0.22 (, library routines will) 430.76 230.33 P
(use the stack of the calling thread; this means that the same routine can) 189 217.33 T
(be called by several different threads at once, with less danger of a) 189 204.33 T
(synchronization problem.) 189 191.33 T
(Another consequence is that if a library routine allocates memory) 189 171.33 T
(, that) 490.69 171.33 T
-0.44 (memory will belong to the owning geode. Thus, when the application exits,) 189 158.33 P
